/* ================= Practice Test 3 · Reading and Writing =================
   Module 1: mixed difficulty. Module 2: an easier (m2e) and a harder (m2h) version.
   Each module: 27 questions, in the official order of question types.
   Researchers, authors and studies named here are fictional unless a fact is common knowledge. */
testSet('t3', 'RW', 'm1', [
  { sk: 'wic', d: 1, ps: 'Because the old bridge could no longer ______ the weight of modern trucks, the city closed it to heavy vehicles.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['support', 'measure', 'describe', 'increase'], a: 0, x: 'The bridge was closed to heavy trucks because it couldn’t hold, or **support**, their weight.', w: ['', 'Measuring the weight wouldn’t be a reason to close the bridge; failing to hold it would.', 'A bridge doesn’t describe weight.', 'A bridge can’t increase the weight of trucks.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 2, ps: 'Biologist Rosa Lindqvist’s findings about tree frogs were initially met with ______: several colleagues questioned whether her sample was large enough to support her conclusions.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['skepticism', 'enthusiasm', 'indifference', 'amusement'], a: 0, x: 'Colleagues **questioned** whether her evidence was strong enough, which is doubt: **skepticism**.', w: ['', 'Questioning whether the sample was large enough is doubt, not enthusiasm.', 'The colleagues engaged with her work closely enough to question it, so they weren’t indifferent.', 'Nothing suggests the colleagues found the findings funny.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 2, ps: 'In her review, the critic wrote that the director’s new film **commands** attention from its first scene to its last.', p: Q_MEAN('commands'), o: ['holds', 'orders', 'leads', 'supervises'], a: 0, x: 'A film that keeps viewers interested from start to finish **holds** their attention.', w: ['', 'A film doesn’t give orders; “commands attention” means it keeps people interested.', '“Leads” is the military sense of “command”, which doesn’t fit a film and its audience.', '“Supervises” is the workplace sense of “command”, which doesn’t fit here.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'The economist’s forecast proved accurate, but its success was largely ______: the outcome she predicted came about for reasons that her model had not taken into account.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['fortuitous', 'inevitable', 'deliberate', 'unprecedented'], a: 0, x: 'She was right for reasons her model didn’t include, so the success came about by luck: it was **fortuitous**.', w: ['', '“Inevitable” means bound to happen, but her model didn’t capture the real causes, so its success wasn’t guaranteed.', '“Deliberate” means intended, but the outcome happened for reasons she hadn’t planned for.', '“Unprecedented” means never seen before, which isn’t the point; the point is that the success was luck.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 1, ps: 'Many people picture glaciers as motionless masses of ice. In fact, glaciers are always moving: the weight of the ice causes it to flow slowly downhill, carrying rocks and soil along with it. Over thousands of years, this movement can carve out entire valleys.', p: Q_PURP, o: ['To correct a common idea about glaciers and describe an effect of their movement', 'To explain why glaciers are melting faster than before', 'To compare glaciers with rivers', 'To argue that valleys formed by glaciers should be protected'], a: 0, x: 'The text sets up a common belief (glaciers are motionless), corrects it (“In fact, glaciers are always moving”) and then describes what the movement does: carve valleys.', w: ['', 'Melting isn’t mentioned.', 'Rivers aren’t mentioned.', 'The text doesn’t argue for protecting anything.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 3, ps: 'Architect Lina Bo Bardi designed the building of the São Paulo Museum of Art, which opened in 1968. ==Rather than hanging the paintings on walls, she mounted each one on a clear glass panel set in a concrete block, so that visitors could walk freely among them.== Some visitors found the arrangement disorienting, but Bo Bardi intended it to free artworks from the order imposed by gallery walls, letting viewers find their own paths and make their own comparisons.', p: Q_FUNC, o: ['It describes an unusual design choice whose purpose the text goes on to explain.', 'It offers evidence that the visitors’ reaction to the museum was justified.', 'It explains why the museum’s building was made of concrete.', 'It contrasts Bo Bardi’s approach with that of other architects of her time.'], a: 0, x: 'The underlined sentence describes the glass panels, and the last sentence explains why Bo Bardi chose them.', w: ['', 'The sentence describes the design before any reaction is mentioned, and the text goes on to defend the design.', 'Only the blocks holding the panels are concrete; the sentence is about how the paintings were displayed.', 'No other architects are mentioned.'] },
  { sk: 'ctc', d: 3, ps: '**Text 1**\nPsychologists have long treated mind-wandering as a failure of attention. Studies link it to missed road signs, weaker reading comprehension and more errors at work, and many training programs aim to reduce it.\n\n**Text 2**\nIn a study led by psychologist Dana Okoye, participants were given a creative problem, then spent twelve minutes on either a demanding memory task or an easy, repetitive one. Those who did the easy task reported more mind-wandering, and when they returned to the problem, they produced more original solutions than those who did the demanding task.', p: 'Based on the texts, how would Okoye most likely respond to the view of mind-wandering presented in Text 1?', o: ['By suggesting that mind-wandering may have benefits in some situations, even if it harms performance on tasks that require focus', 'By agreeing that mind-wandering should be reduced in every situation', 'By arguing that easy, repetitive tasks make people less creative', 'By claiming that mind-wandering improves performance on demanding tasks'], a: 0, x: 'Okoye’s study found that mind-wandering during an easy task was followed by more creative solutions, so she would add a possible benefit to Text 1’s purely negative view without denying its costs.', w: ['', 'Her results point to a benefit, so she wouldn’t say it should always be reduced.', 'People who did the easy task produced more original solutions, not fewer.', 'The study doesn’t show any benefit on demanding tasks; the benefit came after the easy task.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 1, ps: 'Sea stars can regrow arms that they lose to predators. Some species can even grow an entire new body from a single detached arm, but in many species this happens only if the arm still has part of the central disk, the sea star’s middle, attached.', p: 'According to the text, what is needed for many sea stars to grow a new body from a single arm?', o: ['The arm must still have part of the central disk attached.', 'The arm must have been lost to a predator.', 'The sea star must be very young.', 'The arm must stay attached to the original body.'], a: 0, x: 'The text says this happens in many species “only if the arm still has part of the central disk… attached.”', w: ['', 'Predators are mentioned as a cause of losing arms, not as a requirement for regrowth.', 'Age isn’t mentioned.', 'The text is about a detached arm growing a new body.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 3, ps: 'The house had been loud for as long as Mira could remember: her brothers arguing over the television, the radio murmuring in the kitchen, the dog barking at every passing car. The week after her youngest brother left for college, she caught herself switching on the radio just to hear something, then switching it off again a minute later, unsure whether the quiet was a relief or a loss.', p: Q_MAIN, o: ['Mira is uncertain how she feels about the new quiet in her home after her brother leaves.', 'Mira is relieved that her home has finally become quiet.', 'Mira is annoyed that the radio is always on in the kitchen.', 'Mira misses the dog, which has stopped barking.'], a: 0, x: 'Turning the radio on and off, “unsure whether the quiet was a relief or a loss,” shows mixed feelings about the change.', w: ['', 'She isn’t sure the quiet is a relief; she also wonders if it’s a loss.', 'She turns the radio on herself to hear something, so she isn’t annoyed by it.', 'The dog is part of her memory of the noise; nothing says it stopped barking.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 2, ps: 'In the novel *The Long Field*, the narrator portrays her uncle as someone who hides his kindness behind a gruff manner.', p: 'Which quotation from the novel most effectively illustrates the claim?', o: ['“He grumbled that the stray cat was a nuisance, then left a saucer of milk on the porch every night when he thought no one was watching.”', '“My uncle rarely spoke at dinner, and when he did, it was usually about the weather.”', '“He was generous with everyone in town and was proud to be known for it.”', '“He had farmed the same field for forty years and knew every stone in it.”'], a: 0, x: 'Grumbling about the cat shows the gruff manner, and secretly feeding it shows the hidden kindness. Only this quotation shows both.', w: ['', 'Being quiet shows gruffness at most; there’s no kindness in it.', 'He is openly proud of his generosity, so he isn’t hiding his kindness.', 'Knowing his field well shows neither gruffness nor kindness.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 3, ps: 'Ecologist Priya Raman hypothesizes that urban foxes are bolder than rural foxes because city foxes are frequently near people without being harmed, and so gradually lose their fear.', p: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support Raman’s hypothesis?', o: ['Rural foxes moved to a park where people regularly walked but never harmed them became noticeably bolder within a year.', 'Urban foxes have slightly larger skulls than rural foxes.', 'Urban foxes are bolder than rural foxes even in cities where people often hunt foxes.', 'Rural foxes eat a wider variety of foods than urban foxes do.'], a: 0, x: 'Foxes that became bolder after living near harmless people show exactly the cause Raman proposes.', w: ['', 'Skull size doesn’t show that contact with harmless people causes boldness.', 'If foxes are bold even where people harm them, harmless contact isn’t needed, which weakens the hypothesis.', 'Diet says nothing about boldness or contact with people.'] },
  { sk: 'coeq', d: 2, ps: 'A student surveyed home gardeners in four regions about which vegetables they grow. The student concluded that tomatoes were the most commonly grown of the three vegetables in every region: ______', ch: { t: 'table', title: 'Percent of gardeners growing each vegetable', head: ['Region', 'Tomatoes', 'Peppers', 'Lettuce'], rows: [['North', '64%', '38%', '52%'], ['South', '71%', '55%', '30%'], ['East', '58%', '41%', '49%'], ['West', '67%', '49%', '44%']] }, p: Q_DATA, o: ['in each region, a greater share of gardeners grew tomatoes than grew either peppers or lettuce.', 'in the South, 71% of gardeners grew tomatoes.', 'lettuce was more common than peppers in the North and the East.', 'the share of gardeners growing peppers was highest in the South.'], a: 0, x: 'The conclusion is about tomatoes leading in **every** region, so the support must cover all four regions.', w: ['', 'This covers only one region.', 'This compares lettuce and peppers, not tomatoes.', 'This is about peppers, not tomatoes.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 2, ps: 'Birds that nest on the ground in open fields often lay speckled brown eggs that are hard to see against soil and grass. Many birds that nest inside dark tree holes, by contrast, lay plain white eggs. Because eggs inside tree holes are already hidden from view, ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['there may be little advantage for those eggs to be camouflaged.', 'white eggs must be stronger than speckled eggs.', 'ground-nesting birds probably lay more eggs than tree-hole nesters.', 'speckled eggs are easier for parents to find in the dark.'], a: 0, x: 'Camouflage helps eggs that can be seen; eggs hidden in a dark hole gain little from it, which would explain the plain white color.', w: ['', 'Shell strength isn’t discussed.', 'The number of eggs isn’t discussed.', 'The speckled eggs belong to ground nesters in open fields, not birds nesting in the dark.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 3, ps: 'Linguist Tomás Herrera surveyed speakers of a regional dialect and found that people under 30 often used a new slang word for “excellent,” while people over 60 rarely did. Ten years later, he surveyed the same people again. The older speakers still rarely used the word, but most of the speakers who were now in their 30s used it about as often as they had a decade earlier. These results suggest that ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['the word is spreading mainly because the younger speakers keep using it as they get older, not because older speakers are adopting it.', 'the word will disappear once today’s younger speakers reach middle age.', 'the older speakers did not understand what the word meant.', 'the dialect is no longer spoken by people over 60.'], a: 0, x: 'The younger group kept the word as they aged, while the older group never picked it up, so its spread comes from its first users carrying it forward.', w: ['', 'The speakers now in their 30s still use the word as often as before, so nothing suggests it will disappear.', 'The surveys measured how often people used the word, not whether they understood it.', 'The older speakers were surveyed again, so they still speak the dialect.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 1, ps: 'The city library offers free music lessons on ______ students can borrow instruments to practice at home.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['Saturdays, and', 'Saturdays and,', 'Saturdays,', 'Saturdays, and,'], a: 0, x: 'Two complete sentences are joined by a comma plus “and”. No comma goes after “and”.', w: ['', 'The comma belongs before “and”, not after it.', 'A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.', 'No comma belongs after “and”.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'The painter Frida Kahlo, who was born in ______ completed more than 50 self-portraits during her career.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['1907,', '1907', '1907;', '1907—'], a: 0, x: 'The “who” clause opens with a comma after “Kahlo”, so it must close with a comma before the verb “completed”.', w: ['', 'The clause opens with a comma, so it needs a closing comma before “completed”.', 'A semicolon would cut the subject off from its verb, “completed”.', 'The clause opens with a comma, so it must close with a comma, not a dash.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'The recipe calls for just three spices that most kitchens already have on ______ cumin, paprika, and black pepper.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['hand:', 'hand;', 'hand, such as:', 'hand'], a: 0, x: 'A complete sentence comes before the list, so a colon introduces it.', w: ['', 'A semicolon can’t introduce a list.', 'A colon can’t follow “such as”.', 'Without punctuation, the list runs straight into “hand”.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 3, ps: 'The first lighthouse on the island was built in ______ ships approaching the harbor at night had relied on bonfires lit by local residents.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['1829; until then,', '1829, until then,', '1829 until then', '1829, until then'], a: 0, x: '“Until then” belongs to the second sentence, which is complete. A semicolon separates the two sentences, and a comma follows the introductory phrase.', w: ['', 'A comma before “until then” joins two complete sentences with only a comma, a comma splice.', 'With no punctuation, the two sentences run together.', 'A comma alone between the two sentences is a comma splice.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 1, ps: 'The scientists ______ their findings at a conference in Lisbon last October.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['presented', 'present', 'will present', 'are presenting'], a: 0, x: '“Last October” is in the past, so the simple past “presented” fits.', w: ['', '“Present” is present tense, but “last October” is in the past.', '“Will present” is future tense, but “last October” is in the past.', '“Are presenting” is present tense, but “last October” is in the past.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 2, ps: 'The bright colors of the poison dart frog ______ predators that the frog is toxic.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['warn', 'warns', 'has warned', 'is warning'], a: 0, x: 'The subject is “colors” (plural), not “frog”, so the verb is “warn”.', w: ['', '“Warns” is singular, but the subject “colors” is plural.', '“Has” is singular, but the subject “colors” is plural.', '“Is” is singular, but the subject “colors” is plural.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 3, ps: 'By the time paleontologists uncovered the nest, the dinosaur eggs had ______ undisturbed in the rock for roughly 75 million years.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['lain', 'laid', 'lied', 'lay'], a: 0, x: 'The verb is “lie” (to rest), whose forms are lie, lay, lain. After “had”, use the past participle: “lain”.', w: ['', '“Laid” is a form of “lay” (to put something down), which needs an object.', '“Lied” is the past of “lie” meaning to say something false.', '“Lay” is the simple past of “lie”; after “had” you need the past participle, “lain”.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 1, ps: 'An octopus has three hearts. ______ its blood is blue, because it carries oxygen using a molecule that contains copper.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['In addition,', 'However,', 'For example,', 'Therefore,'], a: 0, x: 'Blue blood is another unusual fact added to the first: an addition transition.', w: ['', 'Blue blood doesn’t contrast with having three hearts; it’s another unusual feature.', 'Blue blood isn’t an example of having three hearts.', 'Blue blood isn’t a result of having three hearts; the text gives a different reason for it.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 2, ps: 'The first electric vehicles appeared in the 1830s, decades before cars powered by gasoline. ______ by the 1920s, gasoline cars dominated the roads, helped by cheap fuel and the invention of the electric starter.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['Nevertheless,', 'Similarly,', 'As a result,', 'For instance,'], a: 0, x: 'Electric vehicles came first, yet gasoline cars took over: “Nevertheless” signals that contrast.', w: ['', 'Gasoline cars taking over isn’t similar to electric vehicles coming first; it’s an unexpected turn.', 'Gasoline cars didn’t take over because electric vehicles came first; the text gives other causes.', 'Gasoline cars dominating isn’t an example of electric vehicles appearing early.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 3, ps: 'Some critics have dismissed the novel as a simple adventure story. ______ its plot does follow a familiar pattern: a young sailor, a shipwreck, a long journey home. But beneath that plot lies a careful study of how memory changes the stories we tell about ourselves.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['Granted,', 'Therefore,', 'In contrast,', 'Similarly,'], a: 0, x: 'The writer concedes part of the critics’ point (the plot is familiar) before arguing against them with “But…”. “Granted” signals that kind of concession.', w: ['', 'The familiar plot isn’t presented as a conclusion drawn from the critics’ view; the writer is conceding a point before disagreeing.', 'The sentence partly agrees with the critics, so it doesn’t contrast with the first sentence.', '“Similarly” would add a comparable idea, but the sentence admits a point the writer is about to answer.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 1, ps: NOTES + '- Bessie Coleman was born in Texas in 1892.\n- She wanted to become a pilot, but flight schools in the United States would not admit her because she was Black and a woman.\n- She learned French and traveled to France, where a flight school accepted her.\n- In 1921, she became the first Black woman to earn an international pilot’s license.', p: 'The student wants to explain why Coleman traveled to France to train. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Because flight schools in the United States would not admit her as a Black woman, Coleman traveled to France, where a flight school accepted her.', 'In 1921, Coleman became the first Black woman to earn an international pilot’s license.', 'Coleman, who was born in Texas in 1892, learned French.', 'Bessie Coleman wanted to become a pilot.'], a: 0, x: 'Only one choice gives the reason for the trip: U.S. schools refused her, and a French school accepted her.', w: ['', 'This gives her achievement, not why she went to France.', 'Learning French was preparation for the trip, not the reason for it.', 'This gives her goal but not why she had to go to France to reach it.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 2, ps: NOTES + '- Marine biologist Kalani Akana studied coral reefs off the coast of Hawaiʻi.\n- She compared reefs near the mouths of rivers with reefs far from rivers.\n- Reefs near river mouths received much more sediment washed down from land.\n- Coral cover was about 40% lower on reefs near river mouths.', p: 'The student wants to emphasize a difference between the two kinds of reefs. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Reefs near river mouths received more sediment and had about 40% less coral cover than reefs far from rivers.', 'Akana compared reefs near river mouths with reefs far from rivers.', 'Akana studied coral reefs off the coast of Hawaiʻi.', 'Reefs near river mouths received sediment washed down from land.'], a: 0, x: 'Only one choice states how the two kinds of reefs differ, with both the sediment and the coral cover.', w: ['', 'This describes the study’s method, not a difference between the reefs.', 'This describes the study in general and doesn’t mention any difference.', 'This describes one kind of reef only, so nothing is compared.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 3, ps: NOTES + '- The Voynich manuscript is a handwritten, illustrated book.\n- Carbon dating places its pages in the early 1400s.\n- It is written in a script that no one has been able to read.\n- Many of its pages show drawings of plants that have not been identified.\n- It is named after Wilfrid Voynich, a book dealer who bought it in 1912.\n- It is now held at Yale University’s Beinecke Library.', p: 'The student wants to introduce the Voynich manuscript to an audience unfamiliar with it. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['The Voynich manuscript, an illustrated book from the early 1400s, is written in a script that no one has been able to read.', 'Wilfrid Voynich, a book dealer, bought the manuscript in 1912.', 'Carbon dating places the manuscript’s pages in the early 1400s.', 'The manuscript is now held at Yale University’s Beinecke Library.'], a: 0, x: 'An introduction should say what the manuscript is and why it is notable. Only one choice does both.', w: ['', 'This explains the name, but not what the manuscript is or why it’s famous.', 'This gives its age but not what it is or what makes it remarkable.', 'This says where it is kept, which won’t mean much to readers who don’t know what it is.'] }
]);
testSet('t3', 'RW', 'm2e', [
  { sk: 'wic', d: 1, ps: 'The hikers carried extra water because the trail crossed a long, ______ stretch of desert with no streams or springs.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['dry', 'flooded', 'frozen', 'busy'], a: 0, x: 'With no streams or springs, the desert is **dry**, which is why the hikers needed extra water.', w: ['', 'A flooded stretch would have plenty of water, so the hikers wouldn’t need extra.', 'Nothing suggests cold; the problem is a lack of water.', 'How busy the trail is wouldn’t explain carrying extra water.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 1, ps: 'The scientist ______ her results three times before sharing them, making sure every number was correct.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['checked', 'ignored', 'hid', 'forgot'], a: 0, x: 'Making sure the numbers were correct means she **checked** her results.', w: ['', 'She made sure the numbers were right, so she didn’t ignore the results.', 'She went on to share the results, so she didn’t hide them.', 'You can’t make sure numbers are correct by forgetting them.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 2, ps: 'The new museum guide is written in ______ language, so even young visitors can understand the description of each exhibit.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['plain', 'technical', 'obscure', 'ancient'], a: 0, x: 'Language that young visitors can understand is simple and clear: **plain**.', w: ['', 'Technical language would be hard for young visitors to understand.', '“Obscure” means hard to understand, the opposite of what the text describes.', 'An ancient language wouldn’t help young visitors understand the descriptions.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 2, ps: 'Before facing the stronger opponent, the coach told the players to **draw on** their experience from last season.', p: 'As used in the text, what does the phrase “draw on” most nearly mean?', o: ['make use of', 'sketch', 'pull out', 'attract'], a: 0, x: 'The coach wants the players to use what they learned last season, so “draw on” means **make use of**.', w: ['', '“Sketch” is the art meaning of “draw”, which doesn’t fit experience.', 'Experience isn’t something you pull out like a drawer.', '“Attract” (as in “draw a crowd”) doesn’t fit using experience.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 1, ps: 'Hummingbirds visit hundreds of flowers each day. As they sip nectar, pollen sticks to their heads and bills, and they carry it to the next flower they visit. In this way, hummingbirds help many kinds of plants reproduce.', p: Q_PURP, o: ['To explain how hummingbirds help plants reproduce', 'To describe how hummingbirds build their nests', 'To compare hummingbirds with bees', 'To argue that people should plant more flowers'], a: 0, x: 'The text explains how pollen travels on hummingbirds from flower to flower, and the last sentence states the result: plants reproduce.', w: ['', 'Nests aren’t mentioned.', 'Bees aren’t mentioned.', 'The text explains a process; it doesn’t tell people what to do.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 2, ps: 'Scientists once thought the deep ocean floor was nearly lifeless, too dark and cold to support much life. In 1977, researchers exploring hot springs on the floor of the Pacific Ocean discovered thriving communities of giant tube worms, clams and crabs. These animals depend on bacteria that make food from chemicals in the water rather than from sunlight.', p: Q_STRUCT, o: ['It presents an earlier belief, describes a discovery that challenged it, and explains how the newly found animals survive.', 'It describes a problem and then proposes several solutions to it.', 'It compares two expeditions to the deep ocean.', 'It explains why the deep ocean is dark and cold.'], a: 0, x: 'Old belief (lifeless floor) → discovery (thriving communities) → explanation (bacteria that make food from chemicals).', w: ['', 'No problem is solved; the text corrects a belief.', 'Only one expedition is described.', 'Darkness and cold are mentioned only as part of the old belief, not explained.'] },
  { sk: 'ctc', d: 2, ps: '**Text 1**\nMany parents worry that video games harm children’s social skills, since games keep children indoors and away from their friends.\n\n**Text 2**\nIn a survey of 2,000 children, most of those who played video games said they usually played online with friends, chatting and planning strategies together. For these children, games were often a way to stay in touch with friends rather than a replacement for them.', p: 'Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the worry described in Text 1?', o: ['By pointing out that for many children, playing video games is a social activity', 'By agreeing that video games always keep children away from their friends', 'By arguing that children should spend more time playing outdoors', 'By claiming that video games improve children’s math skills'], a: 0, x: 'Text 2’s survey shows many children play with friends, which goes against the worry that games isolate them.', w: ['', 'Text 2 says games often help children stay in touch with friends.', 'Text 2 doesn’t discuss outdoor play.', 'Math skills aren’t mentioned.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 1, ps: 'Camels can go for days in the desert without drinking. When they do find water, they can drink more than 100 liters in about 15 minutes, quickly replacing the water their bodies have lost.', p: 'According to the text, what can camels do when they find water?', o: ['Drink a very large amount of water in a short time', 'Store the water in their humps', 'Go for days without eating', 'Find more water by smell'], a: 0, x: 'The text says they can drink more than 100 liters in about 15 minutes.', w: ['', 'The text doesn’t mention humps.', 'The text says they go days without drinking, not eating.', 'The text doesn’t say how camels find water.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 2, ps: 'Every morning, Mr. Alvarez swept the sidewalk in front of his shop, even though the city’s street cleaners came by at noon. When a neighbor asked why he bothered, he shrugged. “The street belongs to all of us,” he said, “but this part I can keep nice.”', p: Q_MAIN, o: ['Mr. Alvarez takes pride in caring for the part of the street near his shop.', 'Mr. Alvarez doesn’t trust the city’s street cleaners.', 'Mr. Alvarez wants his neighbors to sweep their sidewalks too.', 'Mr. Alvarez is planning to close his shop.'], a: 0, x: 'He sweeps every day and explains that he keeps “this part” nice: he takes pride in his corner of the street.', w: ['', 'He doesn’t criticize the cleaners; he simply wants to care for his own part.', 'He doesn’t ask anyone else to sweep.', 'Nothing suggests he is closing the shop.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 1, ps: 'In a short story, the narrator describes her friend Leah as extremely organized.', p: 'Which quotation from the story most effectively illustrates this claim?', o: ['“Leah kept a color-coded calendar, labeled every folder, and planned each week down to the hour.”', '“Leah and I met in the third grade.”', '“Leah loved to tell jokes at lunch.”', '“Leah’s favorite color was green.”'], a: 0, x: 'A color-coded calendar, labeled folders and hour-by-hour plans all show organization.', w: ['', 'When they met says nothing about organization.', 'Telling jokes doesn’t show organization.', 'A favorite color doesn’t show organization.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 2, ps: 'A city official claims that adding bike lanes to Main Street increased the number of people biking there.', p: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly support the official’s claim?', o: ['About 300 cyclists used Main Street each day before the lanes were added, and about 750 a day used it afterward.', 'Many residents said they liked the color of the new lanes.', 'The bike lanes were finished ahead of schedule.', 'The number of cyclists on Main Street was about the same before and after the lanes were added.'], a: 0, x: 'A large rise in daily cyclists after the lanes were added directly supports the claim.', w: ['', 'Liking the color doesn’t show that more people biked.', 'Finishing early doesn’t show that more people biked.', 'No change in cyclists would weaken the claim, not support it.'] },
  { sk: 'coeq', d: 1, ps: 'A student kept track of how many minutes she spent reading each day for one week. She read the most on Saturday: ______', ch: { t: 'bar', title: 'Minutes spent reading', x: ['Mon', 'Tue', 'Wed', 'Thu', 'Fri', 'Sat', 'Sun'], s: [{ n: 'Minutes', v: [20, 25, 15, 30, 20, 55, 40] }], yt: 'min' }, p: Q_GRAPH, o: ['she read for 55 minutes on Saturday, more than on any other day.', 'she read for 40 minutes on Sunday.', 'she read for 15 minutes on Wednesday.', 'she read for more minutes on Thursday than on Tuesday.'], a: 0, x: 'The statement is about Saturday being her biggest reading day; only one choice compares Saturday with the other days.', w: ['', 'This is about Sunday, not Saturday.', 'This is about Wednesday, not Saturday.', 'This compares Thursday and Tuesday, not Saturday.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 1, ps: 'Ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats. In winter, lakes freeze from the top down, and the layer of ice on the surface helps keep the water below it from freezing solid. As a result, fish in these lakes ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['can survive the winter in the liquid water under the ice.', 'must leave the lake every winter.', 'live inside the ice until spring.', 'cannot find food in the summer.'], a: 0, x: 'The water under the ice stays liquid, so fish can live there through the winter.', w: ['', 'The water under the ice stays liquid, so the fish don’t need to leave.', 'Fish live in the liquid water below the ice, not in the ice.', 'Summer isn’t discussed.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 2, ps: 'A grocery store moved its fresh fruit from the back of the store to a display near the entrance. In the following month, fruit sales rose by 30 percent, while the number of shoppers stayed about the same. This suggests that ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['placing fruit where shoppers see it first may lead them to buy more of it.', 'the store attracted many new customers that month.', 'the price of fruit fell sharply that month.', 'shoppers stopped buying all other foods.'], a: 0, x: 'The number of shoppers didn’t change; only the fruit’s location did, and fruit sales rose.', w: ['', 'The number of shoppers stayed about the same.', 'Prices aren’t mentioned.', 'Nothing suggests shoppers stopped buying other foods.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 1, ps: 'The museum opened a new dinosaur ______ visitors lined up for hours on the first day.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['exhibit, and', 'exhibit and,', 'exhibit, and,', 'exhibit,'], a: 0, x: 'A comma plus “and” joins two complete sentences. No comma goes after “and”.', w: ['', 'The comma belongs before “and”, not after it.', 'No comma belongs after “and”.', 'A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 1, ps: 'After the long ______ the runners rested in the shade and drank water.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['race,', 'race;', 'race:', 'race'], a: 0, x: 'An introductory phrase (“After the long race”) is followed by a comma.', w: ['', 'A semicolon needs a complete sentence before it, but “After the long race” isn’t one.', 'A colon needs a complete sentence before it.', 'Without a comma, “race the runners” runs together confusingly.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'The tallest peak in Washington State, Mount ______ is covered by more than two dozen glaciers.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['Rainier,', 'Rainier', 'Rainier;', 'Rainier:'], a: 0, x: '“Mount Rainier” renames “the tallest peak” and is set off by commas; the closing comma comes before the verb “is”.', w: ['', 'The name opens with a comma, so it needs a closing comma before “is”.', 'A semicolon would cut the subject off from its verb, “is”.', 'A colon would cut the subject off from its verb, “is”.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'Some frogs can survive being frozen ______ their bodies produce a natural antifreeze that protects their cells.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['solid;', 'solid,', 'solid', 'solid, and,'], a: 0, x: 'Both parts are complete sentences, so a semicolon joins them.', w: ['', 'A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.', 'With no punctuation, two complete sentences run together.', 'No comma belongs after “and”.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 1, ps: 'The children ______ a sandcastle on the beach yesterday afternoon.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['built', 'build', 'will build', 'are building'], a: 0, x: '“Yesterday afternoon” is in the past, so the past tense “built” fits.', w: ['', '“Build” is present tense, but “yesterday” is in the past.', '“Will build” is future tense, but “yesterday” is in the past.', '“Are building” is present tense, but “yesterday” is in the past.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 1, ps: 'Each of the paintings in the hallway ______ a small label with the artist’s name.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['has', 'have', 'are having', 'were having'], a: 0, x: '“Each” is singular, so the verb is “has”.', w: ['', '“Have” is plural, but “each” is singular.', '“Are having” is plural, but “each” is singular.', '“Were having” is plural, but “each” is singular.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 2, ps: 'The students handed in ______ science projects on Friday morning.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['their', 'there', 'they’re', 'its'], a: 0, x: 'The projects belong to the students (plural), so the possessive is “their”.', w: ['', '“There” refers to a place.', '“They’re” means “they are”.', '“Its” is singular, but “students” is plural.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 1, ps: 'Maya studied for her chemistry test every evening for two weeks. ______ she earned the highest score in her class.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['As a result,', 'However,', 'For example,', 'In contrast,'], a: 0, x: 'Her high score came from all that studying: cause and effect.', w: ['', 'A high score doesn’t contrast with studying hard; it follows from it.', 'The score isn’t an example of studying.', 'The score follows from the studying, so there’s no contrast.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 1, ps: 'Many animals, such as squirrels and hawks, are active during the day. ______ owls do most of their hunting at night.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['In contrast,', 'Similarly,', 'For example,', 'Therefore,'], a: 0, x: 'Owls hunting at night is the opposite of animals active during the day: a contrast.', w: ['', 'Owls hunt at night, so they aren’t similar to animals active during the day.', 'Owls are an exception, not an example of daytime animals.', 'Owls hunting at night isn’t a result of other animals being active by day.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 2, ps: 'To make paper by hand, workers first soak plant fibers in water until they break down into a pulp. ______ they spread the pulp in a thin layer on a screen and leave it to dry.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['Next,', 'However,', 'For instance,', 'Instead,'], a: 0, x: 'The second sentence describes the following step in the process.', w: ['', 'Spreading the pulp doesn’t contrast with soaking the fibers; it comes after it.', 'Spreading the pulp isn’t an example of soaking fibers.', '“Instead” would mean workers skip soaking, but this is the step that follows it.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 1, ps: NOTES + '- The blue whale is the largest animal known to have ever lived.\n- It can grow to about 30 meters long.\n- It feeds on tiny shrimp-like animals called krill.', p: 'The student wants to describe what blue whales eat. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Blue whales feed on tiny shrimp-like animals called krill.', 'The blue whale is the largest animal known to have ever lived.', 'Blue whales can grow to about 30 meters long.', 'Blue whales are larger than any other animal.'], a: 0, x: 'Only one choice says what blue whales eat.', w: ['', 'This is about size, not food.', 'This is about length, not food.', 'This is about size, not food.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 2, ps: NOTES + '- The Nile and the Amazon are the two longest rivers in the world.\n- The Nile is about 6,650 kilometers long.\n- The Amazon is about 6,400 kilometers long.\n- The Amazon carries more water than any other river.', p: 'The student wants to emphasize a way in which the Amazon exceeds the Nile. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Although the Nile is slightly longer, the Amazon carries more water than any other river.', 'The Nile is about 6,650 kilometers long.', 'The Nile and the Amazon are the two longest rivers in the world.', 'The Amazon is about 6,400 kilometers long.'], a: 0, x: 'The Amazon is shorter but carries more water, so the amount of water is where it exceeds the Nile.', w: ['', 'This is about the Nile only.', 'This is a similarity, not a way the Amazon exceeds the Nile.', 'The Amazon’s length is shorter than the Nile’s, so it doesn’t show the Amazon exceeding it.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 2, ps: NOTES + '- Researchers wanted to know whether a short daily walk improves mood.\n- 80 adults took part in the study.\n- Half walked for 20 minutes a day for four weeks; the other half did not change their routines.\n- The walkers reported better moods than the other group.', p: 'The student wants to present the study’s result. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Adults who walked for 20 minutes a day for four weeks reported better moods than adults who didn’t.', '80 adults took part in the study.', 'Researchers wanted to know whether a short daily walk improves mood.', 'Half of the participants walked for 20 minutes a day.'], a: 0, x: 'Only one choice states what the study found.', w: ['', 'This gives the number of participants, not the result.', 'This gives the study’s question, not its result.', 'This describes part of the method, not the result.'] }
]);
testSet('t3', 'RW', 'm2h', [
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'Although the senator was known for fiery speeches, her remarks at the memorial ceremony were notably ______, consisting of a few quiet words of thanks.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['restrained', 'incendiary', 'effusive', 'acrimonious'], a: 0, x: '“Although” contrasts her usual fiery style with “a few quiet words”: her remarks were **restrained**.', w: ['', '“Incendiary” means fiery or inflammatory, which matches her usual style instead of contrasting with it.', '“Effusive” means gushing and overflowing, the opposite of a few quiet words.', '“Acrimonious” means bitter, which doesn’t fit quiet words of thanks.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'Historian Ruth Okafor argues that the treaty’s importance has been ______: although textbooks present it as a turning point, the trade patterns it supposedly created had begun decades earlier.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['overstated', 'underestimated', 'concealed', 'corroborated'], a: 0, x: 'Textbooks call it a turning point, but the changes started earlier, so its importance has been exaggerated: **overstated**.', w: ['', 'Okafor says textbooks give the treaty too much credit, not too little.', 'Textbooks openly present the treaty as important, so its importance hasn’t been hidden.', '“Corroborated” means confirmed, but Okafor’s evidence undercuts the treaty’s importance.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'The committee found the evidence for the new policy **thin**, noting that it rested on a single survey with a small number of participants.', p: Q_MEAN('thin'), o: ['insufficient', 'slender', 'transparent', 'delicate'], a: 0, x: 'Evidence from one small survey isn’t enough to support the policy, so “thin” means **insufficient**.', w: ['', '“Slender” describes a physical shape, not the strength of evidence.', '“Transparent” is a meaning of “thin” for fabric or glass, not evidence.', '“Delicate” suggests fragile or fine, but the committee’s point is that there isn’t enough evidence.'] },
  { sk: 'wic', d: 3, ps: 'The novelist’s prose is deceptively ______: its short sentences and everyday vocabulary conceal an intricate pattern of echoes and repetitions that rewards careful rereading.', p: Q_WIC, o: ['simple', 'ornate', 'obscure', 'careless'], a: 0, x: 'Short sentences and everyday words look **simple**, but they hide (“conceal”) a complex pattern, so the simplicity is deceptive.', w: ['', '“Ornate” means elaborate, but short sentences and everyday words aren’t ornate.', '“Obscure” means hard to understand, but the prose uses everyday words; its complexity is hidden, not obvious.', 'An intricate pattern of echoes shows careful craft, not carelessness.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 3, ps: 'The letter had been folded so many times that the creases had worn through in places. Jun smoothed it flat on the table and read it again, though he knew every word: his grandmother’s promise that the orchard would one day be his, written forty years before anyone could have known how the town would grow around it. Outside, the bulldozers had already begun clearing the lot next door.', p: Q_STRUCT, o: ['It describes a character rereading an old promise and then introduces a detail suggesting that the promise may be under threat.', 'It describes the history of a town and then explains why the orchard was sold.', 'It presents a character’s memories of his grandmother and then describes his plans for the orchard.', 'It contrasts two letters that a character has received from relatives.'], a: 0, x: 'Jun rereads the promise, and the final sentence about bulldozers next door hints that the town’s growth threatens the orchard.', w: ['', 'The text never says the orchard was sold.', 'Jun’s plans for the orchard are never described.', 'Only one letter is mentioned.'] },
  { sk: 'tsp', d: 3, ps: 'Some ecologists have proposed bringing large grazing animals, such as bison, back to grasslands where they once lived. ==Critics point out that bison need enormous areas to roam and can damage fences and crops on neighboring farms.== Supporters respond that small herds can be managed within existing reserves and that the animals’ grazing helps maintain the variety of grassland plants.', p: Q_FUNC, o: ['It presents practical objections to a proposal, and the text then reports a response to those objections.', 'It explains why bison disappeared from grasslands in the first place.', 'It provides evidence that bison increase the variety of grassland plants.', 'It states the author’s own conclusion about bringing back bison.'], a: 0, x: 'The underlined sentence gives the critics’ concerns about the proposal, and the next sentence gives the supporters’ answer.', w: ['', 'The text doesn’t explain why bison disappeared.', 'That benefit comes from the supporters in the next sentence, not from this one.', 'The critics’ view isn’t presented as the author’s, and the author never states a conclusion.'] },
  { sk: 'ctc', d: 3, ps: '**Text 1**\nFor centuries, historians portrayed the Vikings mainly as raiders. Their main sources were accounts written by monks whose monasteries the Vikings had attacked.\n\n**Text 2**\nExcavations of Viking-age towns in Scandinavia have uncovered workshops, scales for weighing silver, and coins from as far away as Baghdad. Archaeologist Ingrid Solberg argues that trade, not raiding, was the main source of Viking wealth. She also notes that the monks who wrote about the Vikings had good reason to dwell on their violence.', p: 'Based on the texts, how would Solberg most likely respond to the portrayal of the Vikings described in Text 1?', o: ['By suggesting that the portrayal relies on sources that may exaggerate one side of Viking activity', 'By arguing that the Vikings never carried out raids', 'By agreeing that raiding was the main source of Viking wealth', 'By claiming that the monks wrote their accounts long after the raids occurred'], a: 0, x: 'Solberg points to evidence of trade and notes that the monks had reason to emphasize violence, so she would see the raider image as one-sided.', w: ['', 'Solberg says trade was the main source of wealth, not that raids never happened.', 'Solberg argues that trade, not raiding, was the main source of wealth.', 'Solberg questions the monks’ motives, not when they wrote.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 2, ps: 'The coelacanth, a large fish with fleshy, limb-like fins, was known to scientists only from fossils and was thought to have died out about 66 million years ago. Then, in 1938, a fishing crew off the coast of South Africa caught a living one. Scientists have since identified two living species of coelacanth, which inhabit deep, rocky waters where they had long gone unnoticed.', p: Q_MAIN, o: ['A fish once thought to have been extinct for millions of years turned out to still be living in deep waters.', 'The coelacanth is the oldest kind of fish on Earth.', 'Fishing crews in South Africa discovered many new species in 1938.', 'Coelacanths left the deep ocean after they were discovered in 1938.'], a: 0, x: 'The text moves from the belief that coelacanths were extinct to the discovery of living ones in deep water.', w: ['', 'The text doesn’t compare the coelacanth’s age with other fish.', 'Only one discovery, the coelacanth, is described.', 'The text says they live in deep waters; nothing says they left.'] },
  { sk: 'cid', d: 3, ps: 'When the orchestra’s new conductor asked the musicians to play the opening passage again, the principal cellist did so without a word. When he asked a third time, she lowered her bow, looked at him for a long moment, and then played the passage exactly as she had the first two times.', p: 'Based on the text, what can most reasonably be inferred about the cellist?', o: ['She is quietly signaling that she sees no reason to change how she plays the passage.', 'She is unable to play the passage any other way.', 'She agrees with the conductor that the passage needs to be changed.', 'She has forgotten how the passage is supposed to be played.'], a: 0, x: 'Her long look and identical third performance are a silent message: she won’t change her playing.', w: ['', 'Nothing suggests she lacks the skill; the long pause shows a deliberate choice.', 'Playing it exactly the same way a third time shows she doesn’t agree it needs changing.', 'She plays it the same way all three times, so she clearly remembers it.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 3, ps: 'In a poem, the speaker expresses both affection for the city and frustration with its noise.', p: 'Which quotation from the poem most effectively illustrates the claim?', o: ['“I love these crowded streets, though every siren / tears the thin cloth of my sleep in two.”', '“The city wakes before the sun is up / and hums until the last bus rattles home.”', '“Each siren tears the thin cloth of my sleep in two.”', '“I love these crowded streets and all who walk them.”'], a: 0, x: 'The claim has two parts. Only the quotation that pairs “I love these crowded streets” with sirens tearing the speaker’s sleep shows both.', w: ['', 'This describes the city’s activity without showing either affection or frustration.', 'This shows frustration with the noise but not affection for the city.', 'This shows affection but not frustration with the noise.'] },
  { sk: 'coet', d: 3, ps: 'A sociologist claims that the rise of remote work since 2020 has caused many people to move from large cities to smaller towns.', p: 'Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the sociologist’s claim?', o: ['Among people who moved from large cities to smaller towns since 2020, the share who work remotely is about the same as among people who stayed in large cities.', 'Many people who work remotely say they enjoy working from home.', 'Housing in smaller towns is often less expensive than housing in large cities.', 'The number of people working remotely rose sharply after 2020.'], a: 0, x: 'If remote workers are no more common among movers than among people who stayed, remote work doesn’t explain who moved.', w: ['', 'Enjoying remote work doesn’t show whether it caused moves.', 'Cheaper housing could be another reason to move, but it doesn’t show that remote work played no part.', 'A rise in remote work fits the claim; it doesn’t weaken it.'] },
  { sk: 'coeq', d: 3, ps: 'A researcher studying coffee and sleep claims that, among participants, those whose last cup of coffee was after 4 p.m. took longer to fall asleep than those whose last cup was earlier, regardless of how much coffee they drank in total.', ch: { t: 'table', title: 'Average minutes to fall asleep', head: ['Coffee per day', 'Last cup before 4 p.m.', 'Last cup after 4 p.m.'], rows: [['1 cup', '14', '22'], ['2–3 cups', '16', '29'], ['4 or more cups', '19', '35']] }, p: 'Which choice best describes data from the table that support the researcher’s claim?', o: ['At every level of total coffee drinking, participants whose last cup was after 4 p.m. took longer to fall asleep than those whose last cup was before 4 p.m.', 'Participants who drank 4 or more cups with their last cup after 4 p.m. took 35 minutes to fall asleep, the longest of any group.', 'Participants who drank more coffee per day generally took longer to fall asleep.', 'Participants who drank 1 cup before 4 p.m. fell asleep fastest, in 14 minutes.'], a: 0, x: '“Regardless of how much” means the pattern must hold in every row: 22 vs. 14, 29 vs. 16, 35 vs. 19.', w: ['', 'This describes one group only, so it doesn’t show the pattern at every level.', 'This is about total coffee, not the time of the last cup.', 'This describes one group only and doesn’t compare the times of the last cup.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 3, ps: 'In many bird species, males sing to attract mates, and females tend to choose males that know more songs. In a population of song sparrows studied by biologist Elena Marsh, males with larger song collections also tended to be older and to hold territories with more food. Marsh therefore cautions that ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['females’ preference for males with more songs might reflect a preference for older males or better territories rather than for song itself.', 'older males sing fewer songs than younger males do.', 'female song sparrows cannot tell different songs apart.', 'territory quality has no effect on which males females choose.'], a: 0, x: 'Song collection size goes along with age and territory, so the females might really be choosing on those instead. That’s why Marsh is cautious.', w: ['', 'The text says older males tended to have larger song collections.', 'Nothing suggests females can’t tell songs apart.', 'Marsh’s caution is that territory might matter, not that it doesn’t.'] },
  { sk: 'inf', d: 3, ps: 'At an archaeological site in the Andes, the oldest layers contain bones of guinea pigs of many sizes and ages. Layers from several centuries later contain bones only of larger guinea pigs, and almost all of those animals were young adults when they died. The archaeologists concluded that, by the time of the later layers, the people living at the site were probably ______', p: Q_INF, o: ['raising guinea pigs rather than only hunting them.', 'no longer eating guinea pigs at all.', 'hunting only the smallest guinea pigs they could find.', 'trading guinea pigs to distant regions.'], a: 0, x: 'Larger animals and a narrow age at death suggest people were breeding them and choosing when to use them, which hunting wild animals wouldn’t produce.', w: ['', 'The later layers still contain guinea pig bones, so people were still using them.', 'The later bones are of larger guinea pigs, not the smallest.', 'The bones were found at the site, which suggests the animals were used there; trade isn’t indicated.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 2, ps: 'Marine biologist Sylvia Earle, who has led more than a hundred ocean ______ spent more than 7,000 hours underwater.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['expeditions, has', 'expeditions has', 'expeditions; has', 'expeditions—has'], a: 0, x: 'The “who” clause opens with a comma, so it must close with a comma before the verb “has spent”.', w: ['', 'The clause opens with a comma, so it needs a closing comma before “has”.', 'A semicolon would cut the subject off from its verb, “has spent”.', 'The clause opens with a comma, so it must close with a comma, not a dash.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 3, ps: 'The skull’s shape posed a puzzle for ______ a large braincase, typical of later humans, combined with a heavy brow ridge seen in much older species.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['researchers:', 'researchers;', 'researchers, such as', 'researchers'], a: 0, x: 'A complete sentence comes first, and what follows explains the puzzle. A colon introduces that explanation.', w: ['', 'A semicolon needs a complete sentence after it, but “a large braincase… combined with…” isn’t one.', '“Such as” would make the braincase an example of researchers, which makes no sense.', 'Without punctuation, “researchers a large braincase” runs together.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 3, ps: 'The expedition’s three ______ a geologist, a botanist, and a photographer—spent six weeks mapping the island.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['members—', 'members:', 'members,', 'members;'], a: 0, x: 'The list of members is extra information closed by a dash before “spent”, so it must open with a dash.', w: ['', 'The list closes with a dash, so it must open with a dash, not a colon.', 'The list closes with a dash, so it must open with a dash; a comma makes the list confusing.', 'A semicolon would cut the subject off from its verb, “spent”.'] },
  { sk: 'bnd', d: 3, ps: 'The ancient Greek mathematician ______ calculated Earth’s circumference with surprising accuracy by comparing the angles of shadows in two cities.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['Eratosthenes', 'Eratosthenes,', ', Eratosthenes,', ', Eratosthenes'], a: 0, x: '“The ancient Greek mathematician” works as a title before the name, so no commas are used.', w: ['', 'A comma after the name separates the subject from its verb, “calculated”.', 'Here the description works as a title identifying the name, so the name isn’t set off with commas.', 'A comma before the name with none after it splits the subject in an odd place.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 2, ps: 'When the researchers tested the new battery, ______ found that it lasted twice as long as older models.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['they', 'it', 'one', 'you'], a: 0, x: 'The pronoun refers to “the researchers”, which is plural, so “they”.', w: ['', '“It” would refer to the battery, which can’t find anything.', '“One” switches to a different point of view than “the researchers”.', '“You” switches to a different point of view than “the researchers”.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 3, ps: 'A number of the volunteers who signed up for the study ______ unable to attend the second session.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['were', 'was', 'has been', 'is'], a: 0, x: '“A number of” means “several”, so the subject is plural and takes “were”. (“The number of” would be singular.)', w: ['', '“A number of volunteers” is plural, so “was” doesn’t agree.', '“Has been” is singular, but “a number of volunteers” is plural.', '“Is” is singular and present tense, but the subject is plural.'] },
  { sk: 'fss', d: 3, ps: 'If the bridge’s designers had tested their model in stronger winds, they ______ the flaw that later caused the collapse.', p: Q_SEC, o: ['might have noticed', 'might notice', 'would notice', 'had noticed'], a: 0, x: 'The “if” clause describes something that didn’t happen in the past (“had tested”), so the result uses “might have” plus the past participle.', w: ['', '“Might notice” is about the present or future, but the testing was in the past.', '“Would notice” is about the present or future, but the testing was in the past.', '“Had noticed” doesn’t form the result of an “if… had” sentence; it needs “might have” or “would have”.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 2, ps: 'The town’s population doubled between 1990 and 2010. ______ the number of schools stayed the same, leaving classrooms increasingly crowded.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['However,', 'Accordingly,', 'Likewise,', 'For instance,'], a: 0, x: 'More people but the same number of schools is a contrast, signaled by “However”.', w: ['', '“Accordingly” would mean the schools stayed the same because the population grew, which makes no sense.', 'The number of schools didn’t grow like the population, so the two aren’t alike.', 'The schools staying the same isn’t an example of population growth.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 3, ps: 'Early maps of the coastline were often wildly inaccurate, showing islands that did not exist and leaving out others that did. ______ some of these errors lasted for centuries, copied from map to map by mapmakers who had never seen the coast themselves.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['Moreover,', 'Nevertheless,', 'In other words,', 'By contrast,'], a: 0, x: 'The second sentence adds a further problem with the maps (the errors lasted for centuries): an addition transition.', w: ['', '“Nevertheless” signals something true despite the first point, but the second sentence makes the problem worse.', 'The second sentence adds new information; it doesn’t restate the first.', 'The errors lasting a long time adds to the first point rather than contrasting with it.'] },
  { sk: 'trn', d: 3, ps: 'Most stars in the night sky appear as single points of light. ______ many of them are actually systems of two or more stars orbiting one another, too close together to be seen separately without a telescope.', p: Q_TRN, o: ['In reality,', 'Similarly,', 'Consequently,', 'For example,'], a: 0, x: 'The second sentence reveals what is actually true behind the appearance described in the first: “In reality”.', w: ['', 'Being multiple stars isn’t similar to appearing as one; it contradicts the appearance.', 'Being multiple stars isn’t a result of looking like single points.', 'Multiple-star systems aren’t an example of single points of light; they contradict the appearance.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 2, ps: NOTES + '- The Great Pyramid of Giza was built around 2560 BCE.\n- When completed, it stood about 146 meters tall.\n- Over the centuries, its smooth outer casing stones and its top were removed.\n- Today it stands about 139 meters tall.', p: 'The student wants to explain why the Great Pyramid is shorter today than when it was built. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['The Great Pyramid, once about 146 meters tall, now stands about 139 meters tall because its outer casing stones and its top were removed over the centuries.', 'The Great Pyramid of Giza was built around 2560 BCE.', 'Today the Great Pyramid stands about 139 meters tall.', 'When completed, the Great Pyramid stood about 146 meters tall.'], a: 0, x: 'Only one choice gives both the change in height and its cause.', w: ['', 'This gives the date it was built, not why it is shorter.', 'This gives today’s height without the cause.', 'This gives the original height without the cause.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 3, ps: NOTES + '- In whistled languages, speakers whistle words instead of saying them, allowing messages to carry over long distances.\n- About 70 whistled languages are known.\n- One is a whistled form of Turkish used in a mountain village in northern Turkey.\n- Neuroscientist Lena Arslan studied speakers of whistled Turkish.\n- She wanted to learn whether the brain processes whistled speech differently from spoken speech.\n- She found that both sides of the brain were heavily involved in understanding whistled Turkish.', p: 'The student wants to emphasize the aim of Arslan’s research. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['Arslan studied speakers of whistled Turkish to learn whether the brain processes whistled speech differently from spoken speech.', 'Arslan found that both sides of the brain were heavily involved in understanding whistled Turkish.', 'About 70 whistled languages are known, including a whistled form of Turkish.', 'Whistled languages allow messages to carry over long distances.'], a: 0, x: 'The aim is what Arslan wanted to find out; only one choice states it.', w: ['', 'This is the result of the research, not its aim.', 'This is background about whistled languages, not the research aim.', 'This explains a feature of whistled languages, not the research aim.'] },
  { sk: 'rsy', d: 3, ps: NOTES + '- The Antikythera mechanism is an ancient Greek device recovered from a shipwreck in 1901.\n- It used bronze gears to model the movements of the Sun, the Moon and the planets.\n- Only fragments of it survive.\n- In 2021, a team of researchers proposed a reconstruction of its front panel, showing how it may have displayed the positions of the planets.\n- Their model was based on inscriptions on the fragments and on X-ray scans of their inner gears.', p: 'The student wants to present the 2021 research to an audience already familiar with the Antikythera mechanism. ' + Q_RSY, o: ['A 2021 reconstruction, based on the fragments’ inscriptions and X-ray scans of their gears, proposed how the device’s front panel may have displayed the planets.', 'The Antikythera mechanism is an ancient Greek device recovered from a shipwreck in 1901.', 'The device used bronze gears to model the movements of the Sun, the Moon and the planets.', 'Only fragments of the Antikythera mechanism survive today.'], a: 0, x: 'An audience that already knows the device needs the new research, not background; only one choice presents the 2021 work.', w: ['', 'This is background the audience already knows, and it doesn’t mention the 2021 research.', 'This is background the audience already knows, not the new research.', 'This is background about the fragments, not the 2021 research.'] }
]);
